{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/config.py` is **799 lines** on current `origin/main` (6a4fd2fbb0), one line under the\nlocked 800-line cap in `.github/module-size-limit.txt`. The CI module-size gate fails on\n`loc > cap`, so the file passes today but the NEXT line added to it — a new section-class\nre-export, a new owned accessor — fails `Verified`, and a contributor cannot raise the limit.\nThe file is also inside the gate's non-blocking near-cap warning band (>= 720 LOC).\n\nCORRECTION to this ticket's original premise: it was filed when Gerrit change 1919 added a\n`CodeReviewConfig` re-export that pushed the file to exactly 800. **Change 1919 has since been\nabandoned** and its ticket closed as rejected, so that re-export is not on `main` and the file\nmeasures 799, not 800. The headroom problem is unchanged in substance (one line), so the split\nstill stands; only the number is corrected.\n\n## Approach\n\nSplit `src/rebar/config.py` along the **config WRITE path** — an architectural boundary the call\ngraph already has, and the same kind of seam `_config_schema.py`, `_config_sources.py`,\n`_config_coercion.py` and `_config_sections.py` were already carved out along.\n\n`config.py` is otherwise entirely a READ/RESOLVE module: repo-root and config-file discovery, the\nlayered precedence stack, the resolution caches, and owned composition-root accessors. Exactly two\nsymbols write a file, and they form a closed call cluster:\n\n* `_emit_toml(data)` — the small self-contained TOML emitter (ADR 0070), whose ONLY caller is\n* `write_jira_config(...)` — the read-whole / mutate / re-emit / atomic-replace writer for a\n  rebar-owned `rebar.toml` `[jira]` table.\n\nNothing else in `config.py` calls either one, and the pair calls only names `config.py` already\nimports from its siblings (`repo_root`, `_discover_project_config`, `_parse_toml`, `ConfigError`,\n`_validate_https_url`). So the cluster lifts out cleanly into a new sibling\n`src/rebar/_config_writer.py`, which `config.py` re-exports.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/config.py` — remove the two write-path symbols; add the re-export.\n- `src/rebar/_config_writer.py` — new sibling module holding the write path.\n- `docs/env-vars.md` — RE-GENERATED as a defensive drift-gate re-run. It records\n  `src/rebar/config.py:<line>` positions for non-literal `os.environ.get` reads and is drift-gated\n  in CI (`.github/actions/docs-gates/action.yml` runs `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py` then\n  `git diff --exit-code`). For THIS seam an EMPTY diff is the expected and acceptable outcome: the\n  recorded config.py read lines (295, 308, 312, 319 in `_env_signature`; 572 in `read_secret_env`)\n  all precede the removed block (589-735), so removing that block shifts none of them. The\n  regeneration is still run and its no-diff result asserted, because that is what the CI gate\n  asserts and a seam chosen differently would move them.\n- `tests/unit/test_config_writer_surface.py` — new test pinning the public-import contract.\n\n## Public-surface safety\n\n`config.py` is public surface. Every name importable from `rebar.config` before the split must be\nimportable after. Verified consumers of the moved symbols:\n\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_jira_onboard.py` — `_config.write_jira_config(...)` (module-attribute access).\n- `tests/unit/test_jira_onboard.py` — calls BOTH `cfg.write_jira_config(...)` and the private\n  `cfg._emit_toml(...)`, so the re-export must cover the private name too.\n\nNo test monkeypatches either symbol, so re-export is behaviour-preserving (unlike the resolution\nengine, whose `_ordered_layers` / `_discover_project_config` ARE monkeypatched on the `cfg` module\nin `tests/unit/test_config_perf.py` — which is why the write path, not the resolver, is the seam).\n\nRegistries checked and confirmed NOT to need updating: `tests/golden/config_surface.json` is keyed\nby config KEY, not by file or line; `scripts/check_config_ownership.py`\n(`COMPOSITION_ROOT_BASENAMES`) and `scripts/check_config_reads.py` (`_PLUMBING`) are keyed by\nmodule basename and classify env-read / `load_config` / schema-field-read sites — the write path\nperforms none of those, so the new basename needs no entry. Both gates are run to confirm.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `src/rebar/config.py` is at most 700 lines, leaving real headroom under the locked 800 cap\n      and below the 720 near-cap warning band. Verify:\n      `[ \"$(wc -l < src/rebar/config.py)\" -le 700 ]`\n- [ ] `src/rebar/_config_writer.py` is at least 100 lines (the module-size floor for a split).\n      Verify: `[ \"$(wc -l < src/rebar/_config_writer.py)\" -ge 100 ]`\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_config_writer_surface.py` exists and passes, asserting that\n      `from rebar.config import write_jira_config` and `from rebar.config import _emit_toml` both\n      succeed and that each resolves to the object defined in `rebar._config_writer`. Verify:\n      `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_config_writer_surface.py -q` exits 0.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_jira_onboard.py` passes UNMODIFIED (its `git diff` is empty), proving the\n      existing consumers of both moved symbols still resolve them through `rebar.config`. Verify:\n      `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_jira_onboard.py -q` exits 0 and\n      `git diff --exit-code -- tests/unit/test_jira_onboard.py`.\n- [ ] `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py` leaves `docs/env-vars.md` byte-identical. Verify:\n      `python scripts/gen_env_registry.py && git diff --exit-code -- docs/env-vars.md`.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_config_ownership.py` exits 0.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_config_reads.py` exits 0.\n- [ ] `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py -q` exits 0.\n- [ ] `make lint` exits 0.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` exits 0."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787181538828175001,"uuid":"a7f1533d-30f3-4319-a074-3c62895bbdcf"}